1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:06,900
In behavior labs, someone still watches
every second of video and labels it by hand.

2
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:11,350
For social behavior, that takes three
to four times the video's length.

3
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:15,150
This is Etho, for Push the Frontier: no
training, just plain-English definitions.

4
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:19,306
On a public benchmark, it reaches 0.58 F1.

5
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:24,650
Behavior scoring sits underneath neuroscience
research and drug-safety studies.

6
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:27,508
Today it takes a person with a stopwatch,

7
00:00:27,508 --> 00:00:31,650
or software you must first feed
hand-labeled examples of every behavior.

8
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:34,242
Etho needs one sentence per behavior.

9
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:38,100
This is a video from the official test set.

10
00:00:38,550 --> 00:00:43,800
I paste the dataset authors' own definitions
of attack, investigation and mount.

11
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:46,200
Muse Spark decides what to track.

12
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:52,350
SAM 3.1 follows both mice, and keeps them
apart even when they're tangled together.

13
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:56,708
Muse Spark turns each definition
into a short Python rule,

14
00:00:56,708 --> 00:01:01,554
and Muse Spark Open double-checks the
borderline moments from strips of frames.

15
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:09,731
Around the models, built this week: a
gateway that caps every dollar and caches

16
00:01:09,731 --> 00:01:15,547
every call, the tracking and identity code,
a locked-down sandbox for the model-written

17
00:01:15,547 --> 00:01:19,826
rules, and the two-step check,
with more than six hundred tests.

18
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,650
Here's Etho beside the expert's labels.

19
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:30,300
This is one of the harder clips: 0.44 here,
against 0.58 across all 19 test videos.

20
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:32,650
Here, mount is where it struggles most.

21
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:35,700
Every label is traceable,
including the mistakes.

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,300
At thirty-eight seconds, Etho says
mount; the expert says attack.

23
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:43,440
The frame check answered "attack",
but a rule in the cascade

24
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:46,700
set it aside, and the trace
shows exactly which step.

25
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:49,162
No label goes unexplained.

26
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:51,150
Disagree?

27
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,600
Mark a few bouts.

28
00:01:52,950 --> 00:01:56,250
Etho rewrites the rule and
shows you exactly what changed.

29
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,674
The researcher stays in control.

30
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:04,450
On the test set, scored once:
0.58 F1, with no training.

31
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:10,650
Supervised methods, trained on labeled data
and given pose keypoints, reach 0.79 to 0.86.

32
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:14,569
Etho works from raw video, in
about the video's own length,

33
00:02:14,569 --> 00:02:17,100
for about eleven dollars per hour of footage.

34
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:24,274
Our biggest surprise: Muse Spark Open, looking
at six frames at low effort, added 0.2 F1.

35
00:02:26,450 --> 00:02:30,800
Same pipeline, new animal: fruit flies,
from one sentence, with no retraining.

36
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:38,226
It's a preview: it hasn't cleared the confidence
bar we set, so there's no number here.

37
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:46,650
Etho runs on Meta's models: SAM
3.1, Muse Spark and Muse Spark Open.

38
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:50,300
It's for every lab that still
scores behavior by hand.

39
00:02:50,850 --> 00:02:51,750
It's open source.

40
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:53,874
Try it live at the link below.
